  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: High pressure remains situated offshore\nof SoCal today with zonal flow and warm temperatures across the\nGreat Basin. Daytime high temperatures will reside in the upper \n40s to upper 50s, which is around ten degrees warmer than normal \nfor this time of year. An upper shortwave will pass thru the \nwestern U.S. tonight from the northwest, clipping northeast \nNevada. This will be enough forcing to produce light rain and \nsnow showers. The best opportunity for shower activity will be \nwithin northern Elko County though any accumulation looks light,\nremaining less than 0.10 inches. Westerly winds will also be \ngusty tonight and into Saturday morning in conjunction with the \npassage of the shortwave. Peak wind gusts will be located within\nElko County. Gusts as high as 40 mph are expected, primarily\nacross the high terrain. Weather will otherwise be fair Saturday \nas a transient upper level ridge progresses through the region \nfrom the west behind the shortwave. \n\nFair weather continues thru most of the day Sunday though the \nnext Pacific storm system will move into Nevada from the west \nbeginning Sunday afternoon. This kicks off a period of active \nweather for next week as a more progressive upper level pattern \nsets up across the intermountain west. The first Pacific system \nthat moves in Sunday will be relatively modest and warm. Snow \nlevels will be around 7000 feet Sunday night with light valley \nrain and mountain snow present. Showers will be situated across \nnorthern Nevada with central Nevada remaining mostly dry. \nSignificant accumulation is not anticipated and valleys look to \nobserve around 0.10 inches or less. Southwesterly winds will also \nbe elevated throughout the region. \n\nThere will be a brief lull in activity Monday before the next\nsystem moves in early Tuesday. This is indicated to be a stronger\nevent than Sunday with widespread valley rain and mountain snow \nmoving in from the southwest. Given the southwest trajectory of\nthe precipitation, the greatest accumulation will be observed \nacross central Nevada. A colder air mass concurrently moving in\nfrom the northwest as the parent upper level trof moves inland\nwill gradually lower snow levels thru Tuesday night and \nWednesday. Snow levels are currently indicated to reside between \n4,500 and 6,000 feet by sunrise Wednesday with the lower end of \nthe range situated over Humboldt County and the higher end of the \nrange over White Pine County. Breezes will also be present. \nActivity clears from northwest to southeast Wednesday. There is \nlow confidence the latter half of the week has global models show \nnotable variation in the evolution of the upper pattern. Though \nthe inclination is for at least light precipitaton to remain in \nthe forecast. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Confidence is high with regard\nto warmer than average temperatures and fair weather thru today. \nConfidence is moderate pertaining to shower activity tonight and \ninto Saturday morning. Moderate confidence pertaining to active \nweather early next week, beginning Sunday. Minimal deviation from\nNBM/WPC forecast.",
